  "response": "For much of the early twentieth century, the origin of photosynthetic oxygen was treated as settled in favor of carbon dioxide. The overall equation of photosynthesis balances equally well whichever reactant supplies the oxygen gas, so stoichiometry alone could not decide the matter, and the authority of leading chemists filled the gap. Richard Willst\u00e4tter and Arthur Stoll supported a model in which carbon dioxide is split to release its oxygen. In their scheme, chlorophyll acted on a bound carbon dioxide complex, the freed oxygen escaped as gas, and the remaining carbon was reduced to carbohydrate. Displacing that picture required three things: a comparative argument from microbiology, a biochemical preparation that separated oxygen evolution from carbon fixation, and eventually direct isotopic tracing.\n\nThe comparative argument came from Cornelis van Niel, who studied photosynthetic sulfur bacteria in the 1930s. In contrast to that focus on sulfur organisms, van Niel based his hydrogen-donor model primarily on studies of cyanobacteria, treating them as the bridge between bacterial and plant photosynthesis. The decisive observation nonetheless concerned the sulfur bacteria themselves. Purple sulfur bacteria release elemental sulfur instead of oxygen during photosynthesis, oxidizing hydrogen sulfide as they reduce carbon. That sulfur deposition indicates what van Niel's analogy implied: green plants use water as the hydrogen donor, oxidizing it just as the bacteria oxidize hydrogen sulfide. The Willst\u00e4tter\u2013Stoll model located the source of the gas in the carbon substrate. This analogy located it in the reductant. On that reading, the oxygen a leaf liberates is the counterpart of the sulfur a bacterium deposits.\n\nThe biochemical test came from Robert Hill. Hill showed in 1937 that isolated chloroplasts evolve oxygen in light when given an artificial electron acceptor such as a ferric salt, and that demonstration came well before 1952, when Robert Hill first described the Hill reaction. Oxygen evolution in the Hill reaction proceeded without any reduction of carbon dioxide, and it was this independence from carbon fixation that produced the 1937 chloroplast result. The preparation thus decoupled the release of oxygen from the fate of carbon. That decoupling was difficult to reconcile with any mechanism requiring carbon dioxide to be cleaved before oxygen could appear.\n\nDirect tracing became feasible once the heavy isotope oxygen-18 could be supplied in enriched form. In 1941, Samuel Ruben, Martin Kamen and their colleagues gave suspensions of the alga Chlorella water and bicarbonate of differing isotopic content and then analyzed the gas the cells evolved. The outcome could take only one of two forms. Either the oxygen evolved in the 1941 experiment matched the isotopic composition of the water supplied, or it did not match the isotopic composition of the water supplied; one of these had to hold, and the two could not both be true. Ruben and Kamen's mass spectrometric analysis of the evolved gas determined that the first alternative held: the oxygen matched the water label. Oxygen atoms exchanged between water and carbon dioxide in the algal suspensions. At least one route of isotopic scrambling therefore operated, and perhaps both did, because oxygen atoms exchanged between water and bicarbonate in the algal suspensions as well. Such exchange left open whether the label in the gas reflected its true source or merely the equilibration of the reactants. That tension was settled by a 1975 mass spectrometry study by Alan Stemler and Richard Radmer, which concluded that photosynthetic oxygen is derived from water rather than from bicarbonate. The mismatching outcome served as evidence for that conclusion, since it indicates that scrambling with bicarbonate, rather than any splitting of bicarbonate, accounts for departures from the water label.\n\nThe cumulative verdict is unambiguous. The oxygen released in photosynthesis originates from water. In other words, water is the source of the oxygen gas that plants evolve during photosynthesis. The route to that conclusion is instructive. A bacterial analogy reframed the question, an isolated organelle showed that oxygen release need not wait on carbon chemistry, and isotopic measurement, refined until exchange artifacts could be excluded, supplied the direct demonstration. Each line of evidence was incomplete on its own. Together, they replaced a model built on chemical authority with one grounded in comparative physiology and tracer kinetics.",
